! Evergreen sweep handbacks, wave two

Verbatim wave-two handbacks of the Luna-6 YIN and YANG spirits (2026-10-18); only Markdown transliterated to memetic-wikitext. YIN read isomorphisms first, YANG clocks and version numbers first; each then confirmed or refuted its twin against the code. A Sol-6 reader verified both and refuted ten claims; its plan and the operator's rulings rest at <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/research/evergreen-sweep-2026`>>, and a refutation there outranks a claim here.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/yin-evergreen-sweep-yin-wave-two`>>

!! Evergreen sweep — YIN wave two

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/yin-ranked-findings-by-inherited-shape`>>

!!! Ranked findings by inherited shape


# ''MEASURED · confirmed-by-both — Timestamped IDs propagate clock authority.'' `newEventId()` in `packages/lararium-mesh/src/effect-record.ts:155-160` and `newChangeId()` in `packages/lararium-mesh/src/residency-actions.ts:320-325` both encode `Date.now()` in base 32 before random material. The latter explicitly says the lineage “MAY legitimately sort by mint time”; neither identity needs clock order when causal links/heads supply succession. Node repeats the pattern in `packages/lararium-node/src/sense-sensorium.ts:71-73`. Replace with random/CID identity; preserve lineage continuity on transfer. Twin’s findings 1–3 confirmed by the opened bodies.

# ''MEASURED · confirmed-by-both — TW5 epoch freshness derives from wall time.'' `mintEpoch()` in `packages/lararium-tw5/src/tw5-projection.ts:133-139` uses `max(lastEpoch + 1, Date.now())`, explicitly to distinguish worker restarts. Use an opaque fresh epoch and a causal/owner relation for stale-message rejection. Twin finding 4 confirmed.

# ''MEASURED · confirmed-by-both — Draft deletion is an expiry decision.'' `packages/lararium-tw5/src/wiki-draft-handlers.ts:87-103` computes a Date.now cutoff, parses stored timestamps, and marks old drafts stale for later cleanup. Replace time-authorized deletion with an abandonment/retirement event; timestamps can remain descriptive only. Twin finding 5 confirmed.

# ''MEASURED · confirmed-by-both — Durable effect records write wall time as event truth.'' `mapActionToEffects()` in `packages/lararium-mesh/src/effect-record.ts:255-263` defaults `timestamp` to a new ISO time. The effect ID/order and causal action link can carry event identity and order. Remove from decision data or label as non-authoritative display metadata. Twin finding 6 confirmed.

# ''MEASURED · confirmed-by-both — Verb outcomes write completion/request/start wall stamps.'' `packages/lararium-mesh/src/verb-tiddler.ts:473-487` writes `completed-at`; the same file’s `requested-at` and `started-at` writes at `541-542`, `578-579`, and `635-636` also need treatment. These are event-state records; causal refs should identify transitions. Twin finding 7 confirmed for opened completion body; other listed writes are in twin handback and remain confirmed by its inspection, not re-opened in this ratchet.

# ''MEASURED · confirmed-by-both — Shared byte equality should live at the mesh floor.'' Browser `sameBytes` at `packages/lararium-browser/src/browser-fleet-join.ts:78` and node `sameBytes` at `packages/lararium-node/src/fleet-join-custody.ts:43` are byte-identical length-plus-element comparisons. Mesh has three `bytesEqual` variants at `packages/lararium-mesh/src/sealed-chunk.ts:262-267`, `slip39.ts:350-355`, and `sealed-writer.ts:203-209`. Lift a `bytesEqual` primitive into `@lararium/mesh`; adapt null handling in sealed-writer. The sealed-chunk version accumulates XOR differences (constant-time intent), unlike early-exit variants, so do not flatten that semantic/security difference into one implementation without preserving its timing property. Node/browser copies have no such distinction. Depends only upward from node/browser; direction remains valid.

# ''MEASURED · confirmed-by-both — Strict hex decode is a mesh primitive; edge normalization differs.'' `@lararium/mesh` `hexToBytes` in `packages/lararium-mesh/src/crypto.ts:125-138` rejects odd length and invalid digits while accepting upper/lower hex. Keyhive `packages/lararium-keyhive/src/keyhive-provider.ts:114-117` strips `0x` then calls its upstream decoder; retain prefix handling at Keyhive boundary. Browser stub `packages/lararium-browser/src/__stubs__/crypto-stub.ts:46-51` omits validation and exists only for stubbing. Share strict decoder for production callers, not stub or upstream-specific normalization. Export already exists from mesh. No dependency inversion.

# ''MEASURED · yours-alone — Persona mount algorithm is already centralized.'' `readWornPersonaMount` in `packages/lararium-mesh/src/persona-vault.ts:272-312` reads the selector and all four anchor pins, refuses incomplete anchor sets, and returns one platform-blind mount. Node `packages/lararium-node/src/worn-mount.ts:9-19` imports it and supplies the fs vault and warning sink. Do not lift a second algorithm; browser should compose through its PersonaVault. Its wrapper’s historical “bug and cure” wording at `worn-mount.ts:4-6` is evergreen residue.

# ''MEASURED · yours-alone — Recovery algorithm already lives at the mesh/Keyhive seam.'' `packages/lararium-mesh/src/recovery-keel-core.ts:95-105` reconstructs the root and zeroizes it in `finally`; node `packages/lararium-node/src/recovery-keel.ts:44-53` injects `runReadmitEdge`. Keep injection: moving Keyhive signing into mesh would invert the graph (`mesh -> keyhive` is forbidden). Provisioning cores likewise reside in mesh; node binds vault storage. The node docblock around `recovery-keel.ts:8-12` narrates a prior design rather than current behavior.

# ''MEASURED · yours-alone — Persona roster operations have shared answers but different storage contracts.'' Mesh `listPersonaRoots`/`personaRootExists` in `packages/lararium-mesh/src/persona-vault.ts:173-188` operate through `PersonaVault`; node `packages/lararium-node/src/node-vessel-identity.ts:804-831` reads an explicit roster including verifying keys and avoids root-row reads. Reuse only if node preserves this one-read roster invariant and richer roster result; not a blind replacement.

# ''MEASURED · yours-alone — Same-name record readers are not an isomorphism.'' Mesh `readRecord` at `packages/lararium-mesh/src/sealed-records.ts:422-453` verifies signed/capability-bound content and returns refusal results. Node `readRecord` at `packages/lararium-node/src/guardian-enrolment.ts:98-104` decodes a guardian JSON row; `packages/lararium-node/src/roll-circle-record.ts:40-55` parses another format and returns null on malformed data. Distinct formats, trust checks, and failure contracts; do not make a shared `readRecord`.

# ''MEASURED · yours-alone — Refusal helpers need domain-specific contracts.'' Browser `isRefusal` at `packages/lararium-browser/src/browser-fleet-join.ts:45-47` tests only object/property presence. Inventory counterparts in mesh (`fleet-join.ts:212`, `content-key-holder.ts:211`) and node (`commands/fleet-join.ts:86`) must retain their precise result unions. A property-presence guard cannot establish an isomorphism. Inventory has many `refuse`/`bare` helpers; counts do not prove one refusal algebra.

# ''MEASURED · yours-alone — `splitHash` shares a character operation, not a projection.'' Mesh `packages/lararium-mesh/src/mirror-paths.ts:25-33` splits mirror URIs whose fragment suffix maps to path segments. Node `packages/lararium-node/src/bag-paths.ts:88-115` splits carrier URIs where a fragment means no file. Keep projection semantics local; a generic string helper is low leverage unless additional consumers prove need.

# ''MEASURED · yours-alone — Digest helpers cross the frame boundary illegally as a lift.'' Mesh `packages/lararium-mesh/src/crypto.ts:142-150` hashes bytes asynchronously through an injected provider; frame `packages/lararium-memetic-frame/src/check.ts:61-72` hashes UTF-8 text synchronously with noble. `@lararium/memetic-frame` has no workspace dependencies and sits below mesh; importing mesh into frame inverts the package graph. Preserve local implementation and differing input/provider semantics.

# ''MEASURED · twin-alone — `LarDoc.schemaVersion` propagates one authored counter through mesh.'' `packages/lararium-mesh/src/base-doc.ts:81-94` types the field and puts `"0.1"` in empty docs; `mesh-palace.ts:530` copies it and line 568 recreates it. Genesis injects `"0.1"` at `packages/lararium-mesh/src/genesis-doc.ts:691`. Remove the authored counter throughout the doc shape and projections; content CID/heads identify state. The claim that this field appears in other generated schema sites remains bounded to twin’s inventory.

# ''MEASURED · twin-alone — Session envelopes require an authored numeric schema selector.'' `packages/lararium-mesh/src/session-contract.ts:181-225` requires `schemaVersion === SESSION_SCHEMA_VERSION` alongside owner epoch, session ID and generation. Preserve session ownership/generation checks; remove the format counter or name the actual message shape semantically. This is mesh wire format we own, not an upstream protocol.

# ''MEASURED · twin-alone — Genesis carries contradictory authored version tags.'' `packages/lararium-mesh/src/genesis-doc.ts:243-250` declares `lararium-genesis-seed/v1` while saying seed carries no version, and `materializeGenesisDoc` writes `schemaVersion: "0.1"` at line 691. Remove schema counter and replace numbered authored shape label with a semantic name where a discriminator remains necessary. Twin also cites `engine/v1`, `plugins/v1`, `grammar/v1` at lines 470/500/520; those exact lines were not re-opened in this ratchet.

# ''MEASURED · twin-alone — Browser IndexedDB schema numbers encode additive migration branches.'' `packages/lararium-browser/src/browser-vessel-identity.ts:65,105-111` opens at `IDB_VERSION` and adds stores in v3/v4 branches while retaining prior stores. This is authored local-generation/migration behavior; remove compatibility branches when the store takes the current shape. Distinguish that from upstream wire numbers.

# ''MEASURED · twin-alone — Residency records invite per-record schema generations.'' `packages/lararium-mesh/src/tiddler-store.ts:93-96` documents `schemaVersion` values such as `v1`, `lore-2`, dates, and says no read-time lens projects them. Remove authored format counter if no consumer depends on it; use content identity/causal heads.

# ''MEASURED · twin-alone — Keep upstream protocol versions at the island edge.'' `packages/lararium-mempalace/src/mempalace-client.ts:219-221` sends MCP `protocolVersion` and client metadata; protocol negotiation belongs to upstream boundary. `packages/lararium-mesh/src/sealed-chunk.ts:165-189` uses `keyVersion` as a cryptographic key identity in sealed context, not a package release counter. `packages/lararium-browser/src/admit-carriage.ts:15` names signed `device-admit/v1`; preserve existing signed bytes as a wire commitment. Do not confuse these with house schema counters.

# ''MEASURED · twin-alone — Authored domain `/v1` suffixes are frozen bytes, not a safe mass rename.'' `packages/lararium-mesh/src/domains.ts:29-62` mints domain strings used in signing/HKDF. Existing values bind signatures/derivations; preserve live bytes while adding no new numbered domains. This is an authored version-like naming residue but changing it would invalidate commitments.

# ''MEASURED · twin-alone — Durable records and UI events leak wall time.'' Node tick event writes `{ts: Date.now()}` at `packages/lararium-node/src/lar-event-bus-impl.ts:203`; TW5 alerts use `Date.now()` request IDs and ISO `ts` at `packages/lararium-tw5/src/wiki-behavior.ts:62-80`; mailbox records write `parked-at`/`delivered-at` at `packages/lararium-node/src/vessel-mailbox.ts:49,70`. These fields need causal IDs/state for decisions; retain wall time only as explicitly non-authoritative presentation metadata. Confirmed-by-both for tick/alert/mailbox evidence (twin raised tick and alert; mailbox is this ratchet addition).

# ''MEASURED · twin-alone — Retirement writes a wall stamp into lifecycle state.'' `packages/lararium-node/src/sensorium-lifecycle-verbs.ts:288` writes `retiredAt` with `new Date().toISOString()` beside grounds and prior state. Grounds/event should identify retirement; timestamp cannot authorize expiry. Twin finding 11 confirmed by its cited code; line not re-opened in this ratchet.

# ''MEASURED · confirmed-by-both — Liveness clocks are lawful when they govern only the holder’s wait.'' Mesh `packages/lararium-mesh/src/boot-resolver.ts:213-228` uses monotonic elapsed time and a timer to end its own boot wait; node `packages/lararium-node/src/uds-channel.ts:688-710` bounds a pending response and measures local duration. Retry scheduling in `packages/lararium-mesh/src/sibling-channel.ts:658` only paces a future dial. These do not grant or revoke remote authority. Twin findings 12, 13, 16 confirmed.

# ''MEASURED · yours-alone — Recovery epoch is causal protocol state, not a clock.'' Mesh `packages/lararium-mesh/src/recovery-keel-core.ts:120-145,172-192` receives the epoch supplied by readmit/founding inputs; it does not mint from wall time. Do not remove numeric epochs merely because they are numbers: owner/lease epochs can encode causal succession.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/yin-evergreen-findings`>>

!!! Evergreen findings


# ''MEASURED · twin-alone — Live compatibility arms violate the present-shape rule.'' `packages/lararium-mesh/src/capture/turn-harvest.ts:206` accepts both head forms; `packages/lararium-mesh/src/capture/build-patch.ts:129,190` narrates and accepts legacy/unprefixed sources; `packages/lararium-tw5/src/compaction-handlers.ts:145` retains an old generation read-only; `packages/lararium-tw5/src/meme-ast/builder.ts:116` retains back-compat. Remove obsolete arms and state the accepted present shape, after checking whether any currently supported input still requires them. These have more inherited impact than isolated comments.

# ''MEASURED · yours-alone — Lift comments narrate project history.'' `packages/lararium-mesh/src/persona-vault.ts:2-16` describes lifted control flow; `packages/lararium-node/src/worn-mount.ts:4-6` says “bug and its cure”; `packages/lararium-mesh/src/recovery-keel-core.ts:164-167` narrates future wiring/already-standing behavior. Rewrite as present behavior when code cleanup is authorized.

# ''LEAD · twin-alone — Timer classification still requires callback inspection.'' Twin cites `packages/lararium-tw5/src/wiki-sense-supervision.ts:331` and `packages/lararium-browser/src/browser-gate-pins.ts:71` as possible authority expiry. This ratchet did not open those callbacks, so do not mark them as proven violations. In contrast, browser `guarded-boot.ts:43` is a local teardown budget, and liveness timers above are holder-controlled waits.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/yin-ratchet-disposition`>>

!!! Ratchet disposition


Confirmed from twin by opening implementation: timestamp IDs (twin 1–4), TW5 draft expiry (5), durable effect timestamp (6), completion timestamp (7), tick/alert timestamps (8–9), the listed authored schema/version examples (20–26, 28–29), and liveness cases (12–13,16). Twin’s retirement timestamp and historical compatibility citations were accepted from its inspected wave-one evidence; this ratchet did not re-open every cited line. Nothing in this pass refutes those code claims; the important qualification is that clock metadata can remain only as non-authoritative display data, and signed/upstream version bytes cannot be silently changed.

My wave-one shape findings remain yours-alone where YANG did not address them: byte comparator and strict hex decoder lifts; persona mount and recovery already composed; roster nuance; same-name record/refusal semantics; splitHash and digest boundary. Added mailbox stamps and the sealed-chunk constant-time comparator distinction by reading the implementations. No finding asks node/browser/CLI to import upward into mesh; dependency direction remains mesh at the floor, TW5 above mesh, and node/browser/CLI composing both.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/yin-tail-of-counts`>>

!!! Tail of counts


The cross-package-name inventory lists 68 shared-name rows (inventory itself, not 68 isomorphisms); `refuse` has 20 listed definitions, `bare` 12, `readBody` 3, `isRefusal` 4, `hexToBytes` 4, `sameBytes` 4, `bytesEqual` 4, `sha256Hex` 2, `splitHash` 2, and `readRecord` 3. Counts indicate search surface, not shared semantics. This ratchet re-opened the cited high-rank clock, version, codec, comparator, mount and recovery implementations; remaining noisy inventory hits stay leads. No suites, builds, or git writes ran. Only `out.md` in the handoff directory was written.

<<~/ahu>>

