<<~ ahu slot=`#/yang-ranked-findings`>>

!!! Ranked findings


# ''MEASURED — ID — yours-alone.'' `packages/lararium-mesh/src/effect-record.ts:154-160` and `packages/lararium-mesh/src/residency-actions.ts:316-323` mint timestamp-prefixed random IDs (`newEventId`, `newChangeId`). The latter comment explicitly says lineage IDs may sort by mint time. Remove clock ordering from identity; use opaque random identity and let causal links/heads carry order. The two constructors duplicate one identity shape; a shared mesh mint could replace both if their distinct event-versus-lineage namespaces remain explicit.

# ''MEASURED — ID — yours-alone.'' `packages/lararium-node/src/sense-sensorium.ts:71-73` similarly embeds `Date.now()` in a sensorium ID. Use opaque random identity; causal record/head links name succession.

# ''MEASURED — DECIDES — yours-alone.'' `packages/lararium-tw5/src/tw5-projection.ts:133-139,776-786` chooses mount epochs with `max(lastEpoch+1, Date.now())`; stale-target rejection therefore depends on a device clock after restart. Use a fresh opaque epoch and establish freshness through the mount's causal relation.

# ''MEASURED — DECIDES — yours-alone.'' `packages/lararium-tw5/src/wiki-draft-handlers.ts:87-103` computes a wall-clock cutoff and removes drafts whose authored timestamps predate it. Expiry belongs to an explicit causal retire/abandon act; timestamps may only describe display time.

# ''MEASURED — WRITTEN-AS-TRUTH — yours-alone.'' `packages/lararium-mesh/src/effect-record.ts:232-263` places current ISO time in durable effect records. The effect's causal position should establish order; omit the field or make it demonstrably non-authoritative display metadata.

# ''MEASURED — WRITTEN-AS-TRUTH — yours-alone.'' `packages/lararium-mesh/src/verb-tiddler.ts:473-487,541-542,578-579,635-636` writes `completed-at`, `requested-at`, and `started-at`. The outcome/request events and their causal links identify acts; timestamps must not become validity or ordering truth.

# ''MEASURED — WRITTEN-AS-TRUTH — yours-alone.'' `packages/lararium-node/src/lar-event-bus-impl.ts:203` emits `{ts: Date.now()}` in a tick event. The scheduler's timer can pace local liveness, but its wall timestamp must not stand as event order.

# ''MEASURED — WRITTEN-AS-TRUTH — yours-alone.'' `packages/lararium-node/src/vessel-mailbox.ts:49,70` writes `parked-at` and `delivered-at`; transitions already carry causal state. Remove clock authority from mailbox records.

# ''MEASURED — WRITTEN-AS-TRUTH — yours-alone.'' `packages/lararium-node/src/sensorium-lifecycle-verbs.ts:288` records `retiredAt` from the wall clock. The retirement grounds/event establish retirement; the timestamp cannot authorize it.

# ''MEASURED — OWN-LIVENESS — yours-alone (lawful).'' `packages/lararium-mesh/src/boot-resolver.ts:213-228`, `packages/lararium-node/src/uds-channel.ts:688-710`, `packages/lares-cli/src/commands/mempalace.ts:239-249`, and `packages/lararium-browser/src/guarded-boot.ts:43` use monotonic elapsed time or timers to bound the holder's own wait/teardown. These do not decide shared validity.

# ''MEASURED — OWN-LIVENESS — yours-alone (lawful).'' `packages/lararium-mesh/src/sibling-channel.ts:658` schedules a retry; `packages/lararium-mesh/src/capture/capture-engine.ts:198-200` paces flush/backoff. Keep as local liveness only; ensure elapsed values never enter durable records.

# ''MEASURED — OWN-LIVENESS — yours-alone (lawful).'' `packages/lararium-tw5/src/modules/lar-root-services.ts:108` dismisses a transient UI alert after a local presentation interval. This does not revoke durable authority.

# ''MEASURED — EDGE — yours-alone (keep).'' `packages/lararium-mempalace/src/mempalace-client.ts:219-221` carries MCP `protocolVersion`; upstream negotiation belongs at the web2.5 edge.

# ''MEASURED — AUTHORED — yours-alone.'' `packages/lararium-mesh/src/base-doc.ts:80-94` defines `LarDoc.schemaVersion` and seeds `"0.1"`; `packages/lararium-mesh/src/mesh-palace.ts:530,568` returns that same field. Remove the house schema counter; content CID and causal heads/edges identify bytes and succession.

# ''MEASURED — AUTHORED — yours-alone.'' `packages/lararium-mesh/src/session-contract.ts:181-225` requires numeric `schemaVersion` on session envelopes. Let the message shape and causal session/owner epoch identify current state; no global version counter.

# ''MEASURED — AUTHORED — yours-alone.'' `packages/lararium-mesh/src/genesis-doc.ts:243-250,280-283,351-354,691` has `lararium-genesis-seed/v1`, rejects a `schemaVersion`, and injects a schema field into generated data. Canon says the seed carries no version (`stable-founding-road.mem:307,593`); use seed CID/manifest CID for bytes and remove contradictory counter/guards. Retain a semantic format discriminator only if a reader needs a distinct shape name, without a numbered generation.

# ''MEASURED — AUTHORED — yours-alone, with signed-byte caveat.'' `packages/lararium-mesh/src/domains.ts:29-62` builds authored signing/HKDF domain strings ending `/v1`. They are not upstream protocol numbers, but changing live domains changes signatures/key derivation. Preserve existing signed commitments as frozen edge bytes; mint no new numbered domains, and use semantic names for new separations.

# ''MEASURED — AUTHORED — yours-alone.'' `packages/lararium-browser/src/browser-vessel-identity.ts:65,105-141` uses IndexedDB database version generations and additive upgrade/blocked-version behavior. The house's no-migration ruling makes these authored compatibility branches suspect. Current browser state should identify durable content by CID/head; local store opening must accept its one current shape rather than migrate old experiments.

# ''MEASURED — AUTHORED — yours-alone.'' `packages/lararium-mesh/src/tiddler-store.ts:93-96` carries a record `schemaVersion`; remove this house counter and let record content identity/causal heads identify the state.

# ''MEASURED — UPSTREAM — yours-alone (keep).'' `packages/lararium-mesh/src/sealed-chunk.ts:165-189` uses `keyVersion` as a content-key identity, not a release counter. Canon explicitly keeps content-key versions and places rolls at causal removal acts (`stable-founding-road.mem:182,218`).

# ''MEASURED — UPSTREAM — yours-alone (keep).'' `packages/lararium-browser/src/admit-carriage.ts:15` and `packages/lararium-keyhive/src/index.ts:105` bind `device-admit/v1` into a signed wire discriminator. Keep existing signed bytes as an edge contract; do not generalize it into house releases.

# ''MEASURED — UPSTREAM — yours-alone (keep).'' `packages/lararium-keyhive/src/keyhive-provider.ts:17-19` says Keyhive Archives/events lack a format tag and pins the upstream dependency because version moves re-found vessels. This is an upstream format compatibility boundary, not a house version.

# ''MEASURED — Evergreen and active compatibility — yours-alone.'' `packages/lararium-mesh/src/capture/turn-harvest.ts:206` says a reader “tolerates both” old and new heads; the code accepts both. Retire the backward arm and state the accepted head shape. `packages/lararium-mesh/src/capture/build-patch.ts:129,190` similarly names and accepts an unprefixed legacy source; remove that branch. Both conflict with no migration/shim.

# ''MEASURED — Evergreen and active retention — yours-alone.'' `packages/lararium-tw5/src/compaction-handlers.ts:145` keeps an old generation accessible read-only. Retire this compatibility retention and anchor current reads to the current causal head/manifest.

# ''MEASURED — Isomorphism confirmation — twin-alone.'' Confirmed YIN findings 1–4 and 8–9 by opening the cited lines. `packages/lararium-mesh/src/persona-vault.ts:269-275` owns platform-blind `readWornPersonaMount`; `packages/lararium-node/src/worn-mount.ts:9-18` imports it and binds the fs vault/warning sink. Already composed; no second node algorithm to lift. `packages/lararium-mesh/src/recovery-keel-core.ts:92-105` owns generic reconstruction/readmission; `packages/lararium-node/src/recovery-keel.ts:15-20,44-53` injects the Keyhive edge. Keep dependency direction mesh→no Keyhive. `packages/lararium-browser/src/browser-fleet-join.ts:78` and `packages/lararium-node/src/fleet-join-custody.ts:43` have identical byte equality (`length` plus indexed comparison), also repeated in mesh `sealed-chunk.ts:262`, `slip39.ts:350`, `sealed-writer.ts:203` per inventory. Lift only `bytesEqual` into `@lararium/mesh`; callers' custody transactions remain local. YIN also identifies strict mesh hex decoding (`crypto.ts:125`) as a candidate; Keyhive's `keyhive-provider.ts:114` strips optional `0x`, so retain normalization at the edge.

# ''MEASURED — Isomorphism distinctions — twin-alone.'' `packages/lararium-mesh/src/sealed-records.ts:422-453`, `packages/lararium-node/src/guardian-enrolment.ts:98-104`, and `packages/lararium-node/src/roll-circle-record.ts:40-55` all name a helper `readRecord`, but the first verifies signed/capability-authorized sealed records and returns a refusal union, the second parses guardian JSON and throws, and the third parses roll-circle JSON and returns null. They are not isomorphic; do not lift a generic `readRecord`.

# ''MEASURED — Isomorphism distinctions — twin-alone.'' Frame digest at `packages/lararium-memetic-frame/src/check.ts:61` and mesh digest at `packages/lararium-mesh/src/crypto.ts:142,163` differ in input/runtime contract (UTF-8 text+noble versus bytes/injected provider); frame has no mesh dependency and importing mesh would invert package edges. Keep local. Likewise `splitHash` at `packages/lararium-mesh/src/mirror-paths.ts:25` and `packages/lararium-node/src/bag-paths.ts:88` feeds distinct projections (mirror fragments become path segments; carrier fragments resolve no file); do not merge projections.

# ''MEASURED — Evergreen residue — twin-alone.'' `packages/lararium-mesh/src/persona-vault.ts:2-16`, `packages/lararium-node/src/worn-mount.ts:1-6`, `packages/lararium-mesh/src/recovery-keel-core.ts:164-167`, and `packages/lararium-node/src/recovery-keel.ts:38-42` narrate lifting, bugs/cures, unchanged semantics, or a future absent consumer. Rewrite in present tense during the code pass. In particular, the node recovery comment itself says no live CLI consumer exists, so this is descriptive lead evidence, not a second implementation.

# ''LEAD — Evergreen, yours-alone.'' The archaeology inventory contains many “no longer” hits that state present causal absence, so it cannot justify blanket deletion. Prioritize concrete compatibility branches and retention above; inspect each remaining body before classifying.

